 {
  "slug": "task-automation",
  "id": "6ac89f5186706b45a79b39b6",
  "collection": "6ab2470540448c8f7d1ccbf0",
  "fieldData": {
   "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Task Automation Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is task automation?\", \"a\": \"Task automation is software doing a piece of recurring work that a person used to do by hand, started by a trigger and carried out by a fixed rule. Copying a new hire's details into payroll is a typical example. It suits work that repeats on a schedule and needs no judgment.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you build an app to automate your task?\", \"a\": \"Write the task down the way you would hand it to a new colleague: what starts it, and what should happen after. Paste that paragraph into Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-automation-builder'>AI automation builder</a>, which builds the app and its database. Then test it on last week's real cases before anyone relies on it.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I use AI to automate tasks?\", \"a\": \"Yes, for the steps that involve reading or sorting text, such as naming a document type or pulling a due date out of an email. AI task automation works best when a person still checks whatever the model is unsure about.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the best task automation software?\", \"a\": \"Zapier fits a task that is a chain of steps between apps you already pay for, since a Zap is one trigger followed by one or more actions. n8n fits teams that want to self-host. A task that needs its own screens and data is better built as an app.\"}, {\"q\": \"What do task automation tools do?\", \"a\": \"They start work when something happens or when the clock says so, and they act in another tool according to your rule. Look for task automation tools that keep a history of each run, because that history is where you look when a task fails to fire.\"}, {\"q\": \"Which repetitive tasks should you automate first?\", \"a\": \"Count how often each task happens and how long it takes, then pick the biggest one that never needs judgment. A weekly copy and paste between two systems is a strong first pick. Anything that waits on a manager's sign-off belongs in an approval workflow instead.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you automate work tasks without writing code?\", \"a\": \"Describe the work in plain sentences, exceptions included, and let a builder turn them into logic you can read. With Emergent you change a rule later by prompting. Moving a reminder from Friday to Thursday is one sentence, not an edit to a flowchart.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you automate tasks with AI?\", \"a\": \"Give the AI step one narrow job and a short list of allowed answers, for example sorting incoming files into tax forms and everything else. Run it on 50 old examples first and count the misses. Send anything the model is unsure of to a person.\"}, {\"q\": \"How can I automate my daily tasks?\", \"a\": \"Give each daily task a clock trigger and a rule you can write in one sentence. A 9:00 AM run can sort the attachments that arrived overnight, and a 5:00 PM run can send each owner the items still open. When sorting means reading the files first, pair it with document automation.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is there free task automation software?\", \"a\": \"Emergent is free to start, and the free tier covers building and publishing an app. Before choosing any tool, check what happens when a free plan's monthly run limit is reached, since some pause and others bill extra. Emergent has no task cap.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
  }
 },
 {
  "slug": "interest-form",
  "id": "6ac8a04200f35689bf9ff3c4",
  "collection": "6aaaaa02995bb2f9f4d6a36c",
  "fieldData": {
   "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Interest Form Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is an interest form?\", \"a\": \"An interest form collects the names of people who want something before it is scheduled or built, such as an evening course or a new sports team. Next to contact details, it asks the few questions that show how much demand exists and where it sits.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I make an interest form?\", \"a\": \"Decide what the replies should settle and set a threshold first. Then keep only the questions that move it. For the form with its own database and threshold alerts, describe it to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a>.\"}, {\"q\": \"What to include in an expression of interest form?\", \"a\": \"Ask for a name and email, and put the opportunity in the form's title. Add the answers you need to judge fit, such as availability. An expression of interest comes before a formal application. Keep it short and say when people will hear back.\"}, {\"q\": \"Where can I find a free interest form template?\", \"a\": \"Form builders and template sites each offer a free interest form template, usually for student clubs or volunteer programs. Most give you fields only. Start from the one closest to your case, then add the question that decides whether you go ahead.\"}, {\"q\": \"What to put in an interest form?\", \"a\": \"Put the options you are weighing in the first question, such as three weeknights for a class. Every reply then counts toward one of them. Then ask what would stop someone joining, like price or start month. A league adds age group, and a new building adds unit size and move-in month.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are interest forms used for?\", \"a\": \"Schools use interest forms to see which clubs would fill, and leagues use them to size teams. Landlords build a list before a building opens. Brands gauge creator demand before a creator application goes public.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is filling out an interest form the same as signing up?\", \"a\": \"No. A sign up form books a place in something that already has a date and a capacity. An interest form comes earlier, while you are still deciding whether to run it and how big to make it.\"}, {\"q\": \"What happens after someone fills out an interest form?\", \"a\": \"The reply lands in a database and the counts update. Once you decide to go ahead, everyone whose answers match gets the news, often with a link to the registration form where they commit to a seat or a spot.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is an interest form a commitment?\", \"a\": \"No. A reply says a person wants to hear more, not that they will pay or show up. Expect fewer people to enroll than said they were interested, and set your threshold with that gap in mind.\"}, {\"q\": \"How many questions should an interest form have?\", \"a\": \"Fewer than you think. Contact details plus two or three questions that drive the decision cover most cases. Anything you need only from people who commit, such as a payment or a signature, belongs on the later form.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
  }
 },
 {
  "slug": "t-shirt-order-form",
  "id": "6ac8a04200f35689bf9ff3c2",
  "collection": "6aaaaa02995bb2f9f4d6a36c",
  "fieldData": {
   "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"T-shirt Order Form Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a T-shirt order form used for?\", \"a\": \"A T-shirt order form collects what each buyer wants printed: the style, color, size and quantity, along with their name and how they pay. Organizers use it to total every size for the printer before a print run.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I make a T-shirt order form online?\", \"a\": \"List the shirts and their prices, then decide when orders close. Describe that to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a> and it builds a T-shirt order form that takes payment and keeps a running count per size.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I add names and numbers to a shirt order form template?\", \"a\": \"Give each shirt line a text field for the name on the back and a number field capped at two digits. Put the upcharge next to each field, where buyers can watch the line total change before they pay. Ask for names in capitals, spelled exactly as they should print.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I use a T-shirt order form for a fundraiser?\", \"a\": \"Price each shirt at the printer's cost plus the amount each sale should raise, and price the larger sizes from their own, higher cost. Say on the form what the money pays for. Check the orders table halfway to the close date to see whether the run will reach the printer's minimum.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I count sizes from T-shirt order forms?\", \"a\": \"Count by style and color first, then by size within each group. The printer needs a separate total for every combination, such as 12 navy adult mediums. On paper that means one tally sheet per color. An online form that saves each order as a row adds up each combination for you once the form shuts.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a school T-shirt order form include?\", \"a\": \"Ask for the student's name and homeroom, a youth or adult size, and the quantity. Show the price and the date orders close. A good T-shirt order form template for schools also names a contact for size swaps after delivery.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I make an editable T-shirt order form template?\", \"a\": \"Save the layout as a Word document, not a PDF or an image, and next year's prices and colors take a minute to change. An online form is editable too: change a price or add a color, and the next buyer sees it.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is a merchandise order form?\", \"a\": \"A merchandise order form covers more than shirts, such as hoodies, hats and tote bags, each with its own sizes or colors. Bands and clubs use one to take orders before printing. For goods that are not printed to order, a general order form fits better.\"}, {\"q\": \"Should a shirt order form include spare shirts for size swaps?\", \"a\": \"Yes, for most group orders. Add a few spares in the sizes that sell best and record them as one order from the organizer, which puts them in the printer's count. A buyer who picked the wrong size can then swap on handout day instead of waiting for another print run.\"}, {\"q\": \"Are T-shirt order forms free?\", \"a\": \"Printable T-shirt order forms are free on many template sites. Building and publishing one in Emergent costs nothing on the free tier. When the shirt comes with a race entry, ask for the size on the registration form instead of a separate one.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
  }
 },
 {
  "slug": "event-landing-page",
  "id": "6ac89f545408b01694dc164b",
  "collection": "6aaaa937fe1a8d180b7c9f83",
  "fieldData": {
   "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Event Page Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is an event landing page?\", \"a\": \"An event landing page is a single page built to get people to one event, such as a conference or a launch party. It gives the date and the venue, makes the case for coming and takes registrations through one form.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I build a landing page for an event?\", \"a\": \"Decide what each registrant has to tell you, then write down the date and the venue. With Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-landing-page-builder'>AI landing page builder</a>, you describe the event in a prompt. Emergent builds the page and the registration form, plus the guest list table behind them.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are good event landing page examples?\", \"a\": \"Unbounce and OptimizePress both publish roundups of event landing page examples. Dribbble collects event layouts under its own tag. Across the best event landing pages, the same pattern repeats: date and venue in the first screen, one form, real photos of the room.\"}, {\"q\": \"Are there free event landing page templates?\", \"a\": \"Design galleries and builder marketplaces offer free event landing page templates. A template gives you the layout, while the guest list and the confirmation emails still need setting up. Emergent's free tier covers building and publishing the page with those pieces included.\"}, {\"q\": \"What do the best event landing pages include that others leave out?\", \"a\": \"Answers to what guests would otherwise email about: the ticket price, parking or transit, whether food is served, and the refund policy. The best event landing pages place each answer near the form. Accessibility details, such as step-free entry, belong there too.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should an event registration landing page include?\", \"a\": \"It needs the date and the venue, then a form short enough to finish on a phone. After submit, a thank you page confirms the place and repeats the address. The registrant should leave with everything needed to arrive on time.\"}, {\"q\": \"What goes on a conference landing page?\", \"a\": \"A conference needs more than most events: the agenda by day and track, speaker photos with one line each, the venue with nearby hotels and the registration types. The strongest conference landing page examples keep the form one click from every section.\"}, {\"q\": \"How is a virtual event landing page different?\", \"a\": \"A virtual event has no venue. The page trades the address and directions for a join link and a start time in each visitor's own zone. For a single online session, a webinar landing page is the closer fit.\"}, {\"q\": \"What makes good event landing page design?\", \"a\": \"Good event landing page design puts the date and the register button in the first screen on a phone. Use one real photo of the venue or a past crowd instead of stock. Repeat the button after the agenda for visitors who read to the end.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is it better to build separate event landing pages for each date?\", \"a\": \"Only when the dates differ in audience, price, or program. A roadshow repeating one agenda in several cities works on a single page with a city picker on the form. A venue or club with a monthly calendar needs one events landing page listing every date, each linking to its own registration page.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
  }
 }
]